// `com.microsoft.GroupQueryAttention` — decode-step (seq_len == 1) KV-parallel variant.
//
// # What this is, and what it is not
//
// This is NOT an edit to `gqa_f16.comp`. That file is frozen (`.squad/decisions.md`,
// 2026-08-07): any edit — even a comment — moves its `source_digest` and requires a ledger
// reprove. This is a second, independent shader, selected by `translate_gqa` ONLY when
// `seq_len == 1` (a true decode step). Prefill (`seq_len > 1`) is untouched: it keeps
// dispatching `gqa_f16.comp`, unchanged, on the exact code path #72 already proved.
//
// # The problem this fixes (issue #90)
//
// `gqa_f16.comp` dispatches one invocation per (batch, query_head, query_seq_pos). At decode
// that is `B * Nq * S = 32` invocations for Phi-3.5 — and PR #72's own workgroup-packing rule
// (`gqa_local_size`) leaves decode's packing at exactly 1, because packing further would drop
// below `GQA_MIN_GROUPS` workgroups. So decode still runs 32 workgroups of one invocation each:
// 31 of every 32 SIMD lanes in the hardware wave backing each workgroup are masked off, and the
// one active lane runs a fully serial, unbounded loop over every KV position up to the past
// length. MEASURED (`bench/results/real_model_gqa_local_size.json`): `gqa_f16` is 93.5% of GPU
// time at decode with a 1024-token cache.
//
// # The fix: workgroup-cooperative KV-dimension parallelism
//
// One workgroup per (batch, query_head) — exactly the 32 workgroups decode already dispatches,
// unchanged. But now each workgroup has `W` invocations ("lanes"), where `W` is the
// specialisation constant `local_size_x_id = 0` (the same convention `gqa_f16.comp` uses for its
// own packing). Lane `i` scans KV positions `i, i+W, i+2W, ...` — a strided partition of
// `[0, tok_pos]` — accumulating its own partial online-softmax state (running max, running
// sum-of-exponentials, weighted V accumulator). After the parallel scan, every lane's partial is
// written to `shared` memory, one `barrier()` makes those writes visible workgroup-wide, and lane
// 0 alone performs the final merge (the same pairwise online-softmax combine FlashAttention-2 /
// flash-decoding use for cross-tile merges, applied here at workgroup scope) and writes the
// normalised output.
//
// `W = 1` degenerates to *exactly* the serial algorithm: one lane, one partial, a merge loop
// that runs once and copies its input through unchanged. `W = 1` is bit-identical to
// `gqa_f16.comp`'s decode arithmetic (same summation order, same rescale steps, only a
// pass-through of Q/K/V through `shared` memory in between, which does not compute anything).
//
// # Portability (mandatory — see issue #90)
//
// No subgroup operations, no `VK_KHR_shader_subgroup_*` dependency, no vendor extension, no
// assumption about a fixed warp/wave size. `barrier()` synchronises an entire workgroup
// regardless of how many hardware waves it is split across, which is the whole reason this uses
// a workgroup barrier instead of a subgroup reduction. `MAX_KV_PARALLEL` (16) and `MAX_HEAD_DIM`
// (128, the same bound `gqa_f16.comp` already assumes for its register arrays) size the `shared`
// arrays declared below at exactly 9,856 bytes — comfortably under the Vulkan 1.1 CORE-GUARANTEED
// MINIMUM `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` of 16,384 bytes, so this runs correctly on lavapipe and
// any minimally conformant implementation, not just high-end hardware with a larger limit.
//
// # Numerics
//
// Floating-point addition is not associative, so the parallel merge is mathematically
// equivalent to (same value up to floating-point rounding) but not, for `W > 1`, bit-identical
// to the fully serial reference. This is the same class of difference this project already
// accepts for reordered reductions elsewhere; it is declared here rather than assumed away, and
// it is checked empirically in `tests/ops/test_gqa.py` against the existing correctness
// tolerance.
//
// # Push-constant layout and binding table
//
// IDENTICAL to `gqa_f16.comp` (see that file for the authoritative field list) — this shader
// reads `pc.seq_len` only to size nothing (it is always 1 for a shape that reaches this kernel;
// `translate_gqa` is the enforcement point, not this file), and every other field means the same
// thing. No new push-constant field, no new binding: this is a routing and geometry change, not
// a data-contract change.
//
// # f16 packing convention (shared with all other shaders in this project)
//
//   Tensors are stored as uint arrays, two f16 elements per uint. Reads use unpackHalf2x16;
//   writes use atomicAnd/atomicOr to avoid corrupting the neighbour half-word. This still
//   matters here even though only designated lanes write `present`/`attn_output`: the past-copy
//   path below (`copy_leader`) can have MULTIPLE lanes writing DIFFERENT KV positions into
//   `present` concurrently, and an odd `head_dim` can put two different positions' half-words in
//   the same 32-bit word (see `gqa_f16.comp` step 3's comment for the full argument — it applies
//   unchanged here, across lanes instead of across workgroups).

#version 450
#extension GL_EXT_shader_16bit_storage : require

// Specialisation id 0 — the host's decode KV-parallel factor (`gqa_decode_kv_parallel`).
// Defaults to 1: a pipeline built without the constant runs the plain serial algorithm, one
// lane, no tiling — the same geometry `gqa_f16.comp` defaults to before its own constant is set.
layout(local_size_x_id = 0, local_size_x = 1, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Push constants — identical layout to gqa_f16.comp
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
layout(push_constant) uniform PC {
    uint  batch_size;
    uint  seq_len;
    uint  num_heads;
    uint  kv_num_heads;
    uint  head_dim;
    uint  rotary_dim;
    uint  present_len;
    uint  past_stride;
    float scale;
} pc;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Buffers (f16 elements packed two-per-uint) — identical bindings to gqa_f16.comp
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) readonly  buffer BufQKV  { uint d[]; } qkv;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, std430) readonly  buffer BufPK   { uint d[]; } past_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2, std430) readonly  buffer BufPV   { uint d[]; } past_v;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3, std430) readonly  buffer BufSeq  { int  d[]; } seqlens;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4, std430) readonly  buffer BufCos  { uint d[]; } cos_buf;
layout(set = 0, binding = 5, std430) readonly  buffer BufSin  { uint d[]; } sin_buf;
layout(set = 0, binding = 6, std430)           buffer BufOut  { uint d[]; } attn_out;
layout(set = 0, binding = 7, std430)           buffer BufPresK { uint d[]; } pres_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 8, std430)           buffer BufPresV { uint d[]; } pres_v;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scalar f16 read/write helpers — identical to gqa_f16.comp
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
float f16_qkv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(qkv.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pk(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_k.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_v.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_cos(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(cos_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_sin(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(sin_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
void wr_out(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presk(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presv(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Shared-memory reduction state
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// head_dim <= 128 for all current target models (the same bound gqa_f16.comp's register
// arrays assume). local_size_x (the KV-parallel factor) is capped at 16 by
// `gqa_decode_kv_parallel` — see attention.rs — so these arrays are sized for the worst case at
// compile time regardless of what the host actually specialises.
#define MAX_HEAD_DIM 128
#define MAX_KV_PARALLEL 16

// Q and the rotated new K/V for this (batch, head) pair — computed once by lane 0 and shared,
// rather than recomputed redundantly by every lane.
shared float s_q[MAX_HEAD_DIM];
shared float s_knew[MAX_HEAD_DIM];
shared float s_vnew[MAX_HEAD_DIM];

// Per-lane partial online-softmax state, combined by lane 0 after the barrier.
shared float s_m[MAX_KV_PARALLEL];
shared float s_l[MAX_KV_PARALLEL];
shared float s_acc[MAX_KV_PARALLEL][MAX_HEAD_DIM];

void main() {
    uint lane = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    uint W    = gl_WorkGroupSize.x;

    uint Nq  = pc.num_heads;
    uint Nkv = pc.kv_num_heads;
    uint D   = pc.head_dim;
    uint rh  = pc.rotary_dim >> 1u;

    // One workgroup per (batch, query_head): seq_len is always 1 here, so this is exactly the
    // (b, h) decomposition gqa_f16.comp uses with s_local pinned at 0.
    uint wg = gl_WorkGroupID.x;
    uint b  = wg / Nq;
    uint h  = wg % Nq;
    // Tail guard: gid runs to local_size * ceil(total / local_size); an over-dispatched tail
    // lands at b >= batch_size by construction, exactly like gqa_f16.comp's guard.
    if (b >= pc.batch_size) return;

    uint kv_h = h / (Nq / Nkv);
    int  seqlens_b = seqlens.d[int(b)];
    // seq_len == 1 always for this kernel, so past_len == seqlens_b (see gqa_f16.comp's comment
    // on why the general formula is seqlens_b + 1 - seq_len).
    uint past_len = uint(max(seqlens_b, 0));
    uint tok_pos  = past_len;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Lane 0: load Q/K/V for this (b, h) and apply RoPE, then publish via `shared`.
    // Identical arithmetic to gqa_f16.comp steps 1-2, just computed once instead of once per
    // lane, and staged through `shared` memory instead of local registers.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (lane == 0u) {
        uint qkv_row = b * (Nq + 2u * Nkv) * D;
        uint q_base  = qkv_row + h * D;
        uint k_base  = qkv_row + Nq * D + kv_h * D;
        uint v_base  = qkv_row + (Nq + Nkv) * D + kv_h * D;

        float qtmp[MAX_HEAD_DIM];
        float ktmp[MAX_HEAD_DIM];
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
            qtmp[d]   = f16_qkv(q_base + d);
            ktmp[d]   = f16_qkv(k_base + d);
            s_vnew[d] = f16_qkv(v_base + d);
        }
        uint cos_row = tok_pos * rh;
        for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
            float c = f16_cos(cos_row + ri);
            float s = f16_sin(cos_row + ri);

            float qx = qtmp[ri]; float qy = qtmp[ri + rh];
            qtmp[ri]      = qx * c - qy * s;
            qtmp[ri + rh] = qy * c + qx * s;

            float kx = ktmp[ri]; float ky = ktmp[ri + rh];
            ktmp[ri]      = kx * c - ky * s;
            ktmp[ri + rh] = ky * c + kx * s;
        }
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
            s_q[d]    = qtmp[d];
            s_knew[d] = ktmp[d];
        }
    }
    barrier(); // publishes s_q/s_knew/s_vnew to every lane (barrier() is sufficient for `shared`)

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Group leader / copy-leader predicates — identical conditions to gqa_f16.comp, with
    // s_local == 0 substituted (always true at seq_len == 1).
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint group_size = Nq / Nkv;
    bool kv_write_leader = (h % group_size) == 0u;
    bool copy_leader = pc.present_len != pc.past_stride && kv_write_leader;
    uint copy_dst = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D;
    uint kv_base_row = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Parallel KV scan: lane `lane` visits t = lane, lane+W, lane+2W, ... <= tok_pos.
    // Same per-token arithmetic as gqa_f16.comp step 4 (online softmax, causal), just over a
    // strided subset of [0, tok_pos] instead of the whole range. `copy_leader`'s past-into-
    // present fusion is spread across whichever lanes actually visit `t < past_len`, which is
    // sound for the same reason the single-invocation version was: each lane's write targets
    // are disjoint from every other lane's (different t), and disjoint from the tok_pos write
    // below (t < past_len vs t == tok_pos).
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    float acc[MAX_HEAD_DIM];
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] = 0.0;
    float max_s = -1.0e30;
    float sum_e = 0.0;

    for (uint t = lane; t <= tok_pos; t += W) {
        float dot = 0.0;
        if (t < past_len) {
            uint kv_base = kv_base_row + t * D;
            if (copy_leader) {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                    float kv = f16_pk(kv_base + d);
                    dot += s_q[d] * kv;
                    wr_presk(copy_dst + t * D + d, kv);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += s_q[d] * f16_pk(kv_base + d);
            }
        } else {
            // t == tok_pos: the only other case, because this kernel only ever runs at
            // seq_len == 1 (no sibling-query branch — that case only exists at seq_len > 1,
            // which always dispatches gqa_f16.comp instead).
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += s_q[d] * s_knew[d];
        }
        float sc = dot * pc.scale;

        if (sc > max_s) {
            float factor = exp(max_s - sc);
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] *= factor;
            sum_e *= factor;
            max_s = sc;
        }
        float e = exp(sc - max_s);
        sum_e += e;

        if (t < past_len) {
            uint kv_base = kv_base_row + t * D;
            if (copy_leader) {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                    float vv = f16_pv(kv_base + d);
                    acc[d] += e * vv;
                    wr_presv(copy_dst + t * D + d, vv);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * f16_pv(kv_base + d);
            }
        } else {
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * s_vnew[d];
        }
    }

    // Publish this lane's partial. A lane whose stride placed it entirely past tok_pos (only
    // possible when W > tok_pos + 1) never entered the loop body: max_s stays -1.0e30 and
    // sum_e stays exactly 0.0, which the combine below uses to skip it.
    s_m[lane] = max_s;
    s_l[lane] = sum_e;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) s_acc[lane][d] = acc[d];
    barrier();

    if (lane != 0u) return;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // New-token K/V write — identical to gqa_f16.comp step 3, done once by lane 0 (there is
    // exactly one writer either way; step 3's redundancy argument for `h % group_size == 0`
    // still applies, this just also picks one lane out of the workgroup).
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (kv_write_leader && tok_pos < pc.present_len) {
        uint pb = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D + tok_pos * D;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
            wr_presk(pb + d, s_knew[d]);
            wr_presv(pb + d, s_vnew[d]);
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Combine: the same pairwise online-softmax merge FlashAttention-2 / flash-decoding use
    // to join tile summaries. At W == 1 this loop runs once with l == s_l[0] > 0 and m == s_m[0],
    // so `factor`/`factor2` are 1.0/1.0 and the result is `s_acc[0][d]` unchanged — bit-identical
    // to the plain serial reduction, not merely close to it.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    float m = -1.0e30;
    float l = 0.0;
    float facc[MAX_HEAD_DIM];
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) facc[d] = 0.0;

    for (uint i = 0u; i < W; i++) {
        float li = s_l[i];
        if (li == 0.0) continue; // this lane's range was empty (W > tok_pos + 1)
        float mi = s_m[i];
        if (mi > m) {
            float factor = exp(m - mi);
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) facc[d] *= factor;
            l *= factor;
            m = mi;
        }
        float factor2 = exp(mi - m);
        l += li * factor2;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) facc[d] += s_acc[i][d] * factor2;
    }

    float inv_s = 1.0 / l;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) facc[d] *= inv_s;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Write attn_output[b, 0, h*D .. (h+1)*D - 1] — seq_len == 1 so s_local == 0 always.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint ob = b * Nq * D + h * D;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) wr_out(ob + d, facc[d]);
}
